<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_load.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-load">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-load">
  <primary>LOAD FROM</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>LOAD FROM</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>LOAD FROM</refname>
  <refpurpose>read a table into a Cypher query</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
LOAD FROM <replaceable class="parameter">table_name</replaceable> AS <replaceable class="parameter">alias</replaceable>
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-load-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>LOAD FROM</command> is the relational source clause. It scans a table and binds
   each row to <replaceable class="parameter">alias</replaceable>, whose columns are then
   readable as <literal>alias.column</literal> anywhere later in the query. A graph and a
   table live in the same database, so this needs no import step and no foreign data wrapper.
  </para>

  <para>
   The columns of the alias keep their SQL types. On a
   <type>numeric(3,1)</type> column, <literal>r.score</literal> is a numeric, so
   <literal>r.score &gt; 8.5</literal> is a numeric comparison rather than a jsonb one.
  </para>

  <para>
   The alias itself is a row value. Projecting it gives the row as a jsonb object, so
   <literal>CREATE (:label = to_jsonb(alias))</literal> loads a whole row as the property map
   of a vertex.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>LOAD FROM</command> may open a query or follow another clause, and several may
   appear in one query. It never ends one.
  </para>

  <para>
   The clause has no <literal>WHERE</literal> of its own. Filter with the
   <literal>WHERE</literal> of a following <xref linkend="cypher-match"/>, the
   <literal>WHERE</literal> of a <xref linkend="cypher-with"/>, or a
   <xref linkend="cypher-filter"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Anything a <command>SELECT</command> can read works: a table, a view, a name qualified by
   its schema.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-load-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">table_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name of the table or view to read, optionally qualified by its schema. It has to
      exist and be readable. An unknown name raises
      <literal>relation "..." does not exist</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">alias</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name bound to each row. Read a column as <literal>alias.column</literal>, and the
      whole row as <literal>alias</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-load-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>, which comes with
   the ordinary table <literal>rating</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Read a table:
<programlisting>
LOAD FROM rating AS r
RETURN r.title AS title, r.source AS source, r.score AS score
ORDER BY title, source
LIMIT 4;

        title        |  source   | score
---------------------+-----------+-------
 "Avatar"            | "critics" | 7.9
 "Blade Runner"      | "critics" | 8.1
 "Blade Runner 2049" | "critics" | 8.0
 "Elysium"           | "critics" | 6.6
(4 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The values are quoted because a Cypher <command>RETURN</command> boxes its output columns
   to jsonb, not because the columns are jsonb. Their SQL types are unchanged:
<programlisting>
LOAD FROM rating AS r RETURN pg_typeof(r.score) AS t LIMIT 1;

     t
-----------
 "numeric"
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Join a table to the graph. Load the table, then match against it. The relational column and
   the graph property are compared directly:
<programlisting>
LOAD FROM rating AS r
MATCH (m:movie {title: r.title})
WHERE r.source = 'critics' AND r.score &gt; 8.5
RETURN m.title AS film, m.year AS year, r.score AS score
ORDER BY score DESC, film;

             film             | year | score
------------------------------+------+-------
 "Inception"                  | 2010 | 8.8
 "Interstellar"               | 2014 | 8.7
 "The Matrix"                 | 1999 | 8.7
 "Terminator 2: Judgment Day" | 1991 | 8.6
(4 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Any relation a <command>SELECT</command> can read is a valid source, so a view works the
   same way:
<programlisting>
CREATE VIEW critic AS SELECT title, score FROM rating WHERE source = 'critics';

CREATE VIEW
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
LOAD FROM critic AS c
RETURN c.title AS film, c.score AS score
ORDER BY score DESC, film
LIMIT 3;

      film      | score
----------------+-------
 "Inception"    | 8.8
 "Interstellar" | 8.7
 "The Matrix"   | 8.7
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Build vertices from table rows, naming the properties you want. A graph write reports its
   row count under the tag <literal>UPDATE</literal>:
<programlisting>
CREATE TABLE tmp_people (name text, born int);
INSERT INTO tmp_people VALUES ('Ana Lily Amirpour', 1980), ('Rian Johnson', 1973);

CREATE TABLE
INSERT 0 2
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
LOAD FROM tmp_people AS t
CREATE (:person {name: t.name, born: t.born});

UPDATE 2
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Or take the whole row as the property map, writing <literal>= expression</literal> in place
   of a map literal:
<programlisting>
LOAD FROM tmp_people AS t
CREATE (:person = to_jsonb(t));

UPDATE 2
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Undo both loads, then drop the view and the table:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person) WHERE p.name IN ['Ana Lily Amirpour', 'Rian Johnson'] DETACH DELETE p;

UPDATE 4
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
DROP VIEW critic;
DROP TABLE tmp_people;

DROP VIEW
DROP TABLE
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-load-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <command>LOAD FROM</command> reads. It has no bulk load machinery of its own, no parallel
   writer and no error routing. The <command>CREATE</command> that follows it inserts row by
   row through the graph write path, so loading a large table costs what inserting each
   vertex on its own costs.
  </para>

  <para>
   To load in bulk without maintaining the indexes, run
   <literal>ALTER VLABEL <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> DISABLE
   INDEX</literal>, then the load, then
   <literal>REINDEX VLABEL <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable></literal>.
   See <xref linkend="cypher-altervlabel"/> and <xref linkend="cypher-reindex"/>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-load-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no clause that reads a table. The standard describes a graph
   database, and leaves the relational side to SQL. <command>LOAD FROM</command> is an
   AgensGraph extension that exists because a graph and a table share one database and one
   transaction here.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher has no equivalent either. Its <literal>LOAD CSV</literal> reads a file rather
   than a table and is not accepted; <literal>LOAD</literal> has to be followed by
   <literal>FROM</literal> and a relation name. To go the other way and read a graph from SQL,
   write the Cypher query as a subquery of a <command>SELECT</command>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

